#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "7498df7d-a340-599e-b4ba-496f5142c28c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12379" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the cone $z=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ that is subject to the constraints $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4,\\, x\\geq 0$ and $y\\geq 0$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: x^2+y^2\\leq 4, \\, x\\geq 0, \\, y\\geq 0 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3602/Source/Images/7230d57545aadd33786457bbde003627.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}) = \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}})^{2} + (\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region $D$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\sqrt{2}dA &= ∬_(Δ)\\sqrt{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}∫_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{2}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π/2}dθ∫_{0}^{2}rdr \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot [θ]_{0}^{π/2} \\cdot [\\frac{r^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{π}{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= π\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175751 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/id #uuid "bcdc7b9f-b5f8-5516-a0e6-8ca54fb80a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8948" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x =y+\\sqrt 2 z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by the constraints $1 \\leq y\\leq 2$ and $1 \\leq z \\leq 3$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx = y+\\sqrt 2 z, \\qquad (y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z \\leq 3\\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3602/Source/Images/573dc3290eb86abf9cb97e3dcfc181a6.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂x}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y + \\sqrt{2}z) = 1, \\\\\n\\frac{∂x}{∂z} &= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y + \\sqrt{2}z) = \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂x}{∂y})^{2} + (\\frac{∂x}{∂z})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (1)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{4}dA \\\\\n&= 2∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (2 - 1) \\cdot (3 - 1) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175752 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "80c2abda-a1d9-569e-936e-fc44ba77eb50" :question/math-academy-id "e-8959" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z = y - 4x$ that is bounded by the constraints $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z = f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = y - 4x, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq 1\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y - 4x) =-4, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y - 4x) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (-4)^{2} + (1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + 16 + 1}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{18}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3\\sqrt{2}dA \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2}∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2} \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2} \\cdot (2 - 0) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175770 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "77e8fb3e-1858-5831-b740-a0c133c8180f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9141" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the plane $z = 2y+2x$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\cos{x}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = 2y+2x, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\cos{x} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3602/Source/Images/8a755284c1de4b4761f2aaeff802f7d4.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y + 2x) = 2, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y + 2x) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (2)^{2} + (2)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3dA \\\\\n&= 3∫_{0}^{π/2}∫_{0}^{\\cos x}dydx \\\\\n&= 3∫_{0}^{π/2}y\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{\\cos x}dx \\\\ = 3∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos xdx \\\\ = 3\\sin x\\end{vmatrix}_{0}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= 3(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1b43b1a-1d4f-5e1d-b50b-29e40a4f4372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(π\\sqrt{π} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173479} {:answer/id #uuid "c2940e97-59ad-57d0-96ce-e6ecc4cb22c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173480} {:answer/id #uuid "18697c0c-2948-5dbd-a93f-274a2fc87324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173481} {:answer/id #uuid "29d42868-da7f-5103-862d-04bb0580ab84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173482} {:answer/id #uuid "58a21276-f065-5541-9ce2-4051a2c0741a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58a21276-f065-5541-9ce2-4051a2c0741a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173483} :answer-field/id #uuid "f987312d-9a9b-5513-8911-d05b339c27a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173478}] :question/id #uuid "40e44b1f-1930-5622-81b2-291ea09677cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106034" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y = \\sin x + z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z = \\sin (2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af81d187-390f-52a8-b640-67ff829dba62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173647} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a6aa28-4251-574a-9e9c-9dd91a4e6a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173648} {:answer/id #uuid "c767c49a-114b-5d17-8f38-a90bceee4fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173649} {:answer/id #uuid "f8201991-cdf5-5fa6-8942-7709c6a1f656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answ